    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q131">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q138"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1390"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1624"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1633"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1855"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1856"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1857"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1860"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1868"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1869"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1873"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1831"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q786"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q787"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1311">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1312"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q643"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1817"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1312">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1311"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q132">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1845"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1853"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q787"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Logging techniques that result in significantly less damage to the surrounding forest and forest ecosystem. Examples of reduced impact logging include directional felling, trimming of inter-crown vines, and careful road planning. Reduced impact logging helps to maintain forest ecosystem integrity and resilience to climate change.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1320">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1315"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q661"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1339">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1337"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q779"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q134">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1846"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1880"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1853"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q787"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Forested catchments protection refers to the practice of preserving and managing forested areas within a catchment (or watershed) to maintain the health and function of the entire ecosystem. A catchment is the area of land where all precipitation collects and drains into a common outlet, such as a river, lake, or reservoir.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1344">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1343"/>
        <cpr:P20 xml:lang="en">c6aeqbzf</cpr:P20>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Climate finance encompasses financial actors and resources primarily directed towards climate change mitigation, adaptation and compensation. It is characterised by its specific intent to address the challenges and impacts of climate change. Mitigation finance is specifically allocated to activities that aim to reduce greenhouse gas emissions.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1345">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1343"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1830"/>
        <cpr:P20 xml:lang="en">r4qxcp34</cpr:P20>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1585"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Climate finance encompasses financial actors and resources primarily directed towards climate change mitigation, adaptation and compensation. It is characterised by its specific intent to address the challenges and impacts of climate change. Adaptation finance is specifically allocated to activities that enhance resilience to climate impacts.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1346">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1343"/>
        <cpr:P20 xml:lang="en">aqrcdtxx</cpr:P20>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Climate finance encompasses financial actors and resources primarily directed towards climate change mitigation, adaptation and compensation. It is characterised by its specific intent to address the challenges and impacts of climate change. Loss and damage finance refers to financial mechanisms that cover the significant and often unavoidable economic costs from climate change impacts that remain even after the most effective adaptation measures are applied, particularly in vulnerable regions, as illustrated by integrated economic models that compare optimal adaptation spending with the persistent residual damages.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q135">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1846"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Buffer zones are designated areas used to protect sensitive landscape patches (e.g., wetlands, wildlife reserves) to lessen the impacts of human activity and land disturbance.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q136">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q136"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1846"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q137"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q140"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q422"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Wildfire control refers to efforts and strategies aimed at minimizing the risk, frequency, and severity of wildfires. One strategy is the &apos;firebreaks&apos;, which purpose is to prevent forest fires from spreading before they damage the ecosystem, cropland or personal property. It consists in creating cleared areas to stop or slow the spread of wildfires.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1362">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1363"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1364"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1365"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1366"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1342"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1343"/>
        <cpr:P20 xml:lang="en">bugd7dbt</cpr:P20>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">A dedicated financial mechanism that mobilises, manages and disburses resources to support actions addressing climate change. Climate funds are designed to facilitate the transition to a low-carbon, climate-resilient future by financing projects and initiatives that reduce greenhouse gas emissions (mitigation), help societies adapt to the impacts of climate change (adaptation) and pay for the economic harm from climate change that cannot be completely prevented by adaptation measures (loss and damage). Climate funds can be public, private or a blend of both but this concept is focused on public climate finance. They may operate at international, national or local levels but this concept is focused on international and national climate finance flows. Climate funds use a range of financial instruments and are often structured to attract additional investment from governments, development banks, businesses and other stakeholders. Their governance, priorities and funding mechanisms vary, but all climate funds share the common goal of accelerating climate action through strategic financial support.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1363">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1362"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1361"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1364"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1583"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">A financial mechanism that pools resources from multiple donor countries and institutions to support climate change mitigation and adaptation projects across developing nations. Typically managed by international organisations or development banks, a multilateral climate fund operates with established governance structures and funding criteria.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1364">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1362"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1363"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">An organisation that has been formally approved by a climate fund to receive and manage climate finance resources on behalf of implementing partners. These entities must meet specific fiduciary standards, environmental and social safeguards, and demonstrate capacity to develop and implement climate projects. Accredited entities can be national, regional, or international organisations that serve as intermediaries between climate funds and project executors and can be accredited as implementing, delivery or executing entities.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1365">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1362"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">A climate financing arrangement where one country directly provides climate-related funding to another country or specific projects. These initiatives are typically managed through the donor country&apos;s development agency or dedicated climate programs and reflect the donor&apos;s strategic priorities and interests. Bilateral climate funds often focus on specific regions or types of climate action that align with the donor country&apos;s foreign policy objectives.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1366">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1362"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">A country-specific funding mechanism established to receive and manage climate finance from both domestic and international sources. These funds serve as national entities that coordinate climate-related investments, ensure alignment with national climate strategies and streamline the implementation of climate projects within the country. They often act as central coordinating bodies for climate finance at the national level.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1368">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1367"/>
        <cpr:P20 xml:lang="en">9n9xsymu</cpr:P20>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Government-backed institution that offers financing, guarantees, and insurance to support domestic companies’ international exports—including funding climate‐related projects by reducing commercial and political risks.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1369">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1367"/>
        <cpr:P20 xml:lang="en">wyaugrjm</cpr:P20>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">National institution that manages a country’s currency and monetary policy, and in some jurisdictions increasingly incorporates green monetary strategies to mitigate climate risks.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q137">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1846"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q136"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q140"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Prescribed fire is a form of land management in which fire is intentionally applied to vegetation. Prescribed fires are conducted under desired conditions to meet specific objectives, such as to restore fire regimes in adapted ecosystems or to limit the amount of dry brush in an area prone to wildfires.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1370">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1367"/>
        <cpr:P20 xml:lang="en">6dxvusb9</cpr:P20>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Company owned wholly or partially by the state, typically operating in key sectors such as energy, infrastructure, or transportation, where they help implement public policies – which may include those aimed at a low‑carbon transition.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1371">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1367"/>
        <cpr:P20 xml:lang="en">v76y24qd</cpr:P20>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Government‑controlled bank or financial entity tasked with promoting social and economic development through initiatives that may include sustainable investments.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q138">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q131"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1846"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1156"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q126"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1516"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q432"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">The fundamental principle of dune fixation is that of preventing sand from moving during a long enough period to allow natural or planted vegetation to become established. If sand encroachment is to be controlled, saltation must be reduced, either by stabilizing the soil or by reducing the wind speed gradient close to the surface of the ground.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q139">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1846"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q741"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Biodiversity management systems (BMS) refer to organised frameworks or strategies designed to conserve and protect biodiversity in a given area, while also ensuring that human activities and development do not negatively impact ecosystems. These systems integrate environmental, social, and economic aspects to maintain or enhance biodiversity. The principal purpose of a BMS is to include biodiversity systematically in all planning and operational processes and amend relevant existing guidelines accordingly.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q140">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2546"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2662"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2663"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1846"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2795"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q136"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q137"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q422"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Wildfire management includes all activities required for the protection of burnable forest and other vegetation values from fire and the use of fire to meet land management goals and objectives. It involves the strategic integration of such factors as a knowledge of fire regimes, probable fire effects, values-at-risk, level of forest protection required, cost of fire-related activities, and prescribed fire technology into multiple-use planning, decision making, and day-to-day activities to accomplish stated resource management objectives. Successful fire management depends on effective fire prevention, detection, and pre-suppression, having an adequate fire suppression capability, and consideration of fire ecology relationships.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q141">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1860"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">In agriculture, terracing refers to the technique of creating flat, stepped areas on slopes to facilitate farming. This method enhances water retention and reduces soil erosion, making it an essential practice in regions with hilly terrains.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q142">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1860"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2554"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Integrated nutrient management refers to the maintenance of soil fertility and of plant nutrient supply at an optimum level for sustaining the desired productivity through optimization of the benefits from all possible sources of organic, inorganic and biological components in an integrated manner.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1424">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1691"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q847"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q768"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q143">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1860"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Soil moisture is the amount of water in the soil. It is linked to the water cycle and is affected by both weather and climate patterns. It is also a vital component of the overall composition of ecosystems, affecting biodiversity. Accurate monitoring of soil moisture is therefore essential for understanding and managing environmental and societal challenges such as agricultural dynamics and biodiversity monitoring.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
