    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1707">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q839"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1708">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q839"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1709">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q839"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q171">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q157"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1635"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Soil moisture conservation aims to minimise the amount of water lost from the soils through evaporation (water loss directly from the soil) and transpiration (water loss occurring through the plants). Preserving soil moisture is important means to maintain the necessary water for agricultural production, and also helps minimise irrigation needs of the crops. This is especially important in areas where rainwater and/or groundwater resources for irrigation are scarce or decreasing due to climate change.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1710">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q839"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1718">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1527"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1528"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1534"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1535"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1719"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1720"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1721"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1726"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q827"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1719">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1718"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q172">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q157"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1635"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q684"/>
        <cpr:P4 rdf:resource="https://atpsnet.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/05/rps22.pdf"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1720">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1718"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1721">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1718"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1722">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q766"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1723">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2747"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1724">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q766"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1725">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q766"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1726">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1718"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1728">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q911"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1734"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">The just distribution of benefits and impacts related to climate change viewed, through a spatial lens. This can mean, for example, offsetting loss of opportunities in an area with additional investments, providing extra protections for highly vulnerable areas, or ensuring that resources and infrastructure are reaching historically left-behind areas. Often, spatial justice is addressing existing inequalities between rural and urban areas, which may increase due to climate change and the green transition; however, other spatial scales, such as under-invested neighbourhoods, may also be considered relevant.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q173">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q157"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1883"/>
        <cpr:P4 rdf:resource="https://www.ctc-n.org/technologies/seawater-desalination"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1730">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q32"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Remaking structural social and institutional inequalities that drive and perpetuate climate change through a whole-of-society transformation. This includes the argument that addressing climate justice requires a fundamental shift away from capitalist, extractivist, and/or neo-colonial systems.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1731">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1732"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q32"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">the recognition of underlying systemic injustices and representation of dignity, values and identities of humans and other species. When referencing humans, this dimension refers both to individuals and social groups and entails recognising and respecting how different values, preferences and needs are rooted in diverse histories, identities and cultural backgrounds. For other species, this implies recognising the intrinsic and inherent value of nature regardless of its utility to humans</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1732">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1731"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">emphasises the importance of fostering collaborative relationships through dialogue and negotiation, arguing that cooperation between groups and communities must purposely aim to advance climate justice. Fair, equal and respectful relationships are seen as essential in developing equitable climate change responses.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1733">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1744"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1813"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q58"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q47"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q912"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Governance approaches that promote justice,at minimum by providing a formal opportunity for widespread consultation, but ideally by focussing on institutionalising inclusive principles, creating the conditions for meaningful participation, such as by addressing imbalances in power, access to justice, or the resources to participate effectively</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1736">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q47"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1737">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q47"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q72"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1738">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q47"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1739">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1764"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q892"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q47"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Creating new economic opportunities to replace traditional industries or update existing ones, while respecting other elements of climate justice.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q174">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q157"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1883"/>
        <cpr:P4 rdf:resource="https://www.adb.org/sites/default/files/publication/149400/technologies-climate-change-adaptation.pdf"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1741">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1763"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">a set of practices as well as a political movement that is radically inclusive, international, class conscious and community-based, to bring about a more equitable world through democratic and self-organised means</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1742">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1763"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Creating a representative group of citizens to learn about, deliberate upon, and make recommendations on a given issue. The group is usually randomly selected and their recommendations can have varying levels of political weight, though they are in most cases not binding.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1744">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1745"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1746"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1759"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1807"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1733"/>
        <cpr:P20 xml:lang="en">7aeap3ca</cpr:P20>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1401"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Certain groups of the population are particularly vulnerable to human rights violations for economic, social, political or historical reasons. Therefore, international law ensures that they receive specific protection.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1745">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1744"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1602"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1757"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q684"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1746">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1744"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q843"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Access to justice refers to the public’s right to review by a court or another independent body to ensure that public authorities respect the rights to access to information and public participation, and environmental law in general.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1747">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1759"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q684"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Self-determination is an ongoing process of ensuring that peoples are able to make decisions about matters that affect their lives, based on choice, participation and control. Articles 3 and 4 of the United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples (UNDRIP) confirms that Indigenous peoples have the right to self-determination under international law. By virtue of that right they freely determine their political status and freely pursue their economic, social and cultural development.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1748">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1759"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1602"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1758"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q175">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q157"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1883"/>
        <cpr:P4 rdf:resource="https://www.ctc-n.org/resources/solar-water-distillation"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1750">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1254"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Non-methane volatile organic compounds (NMVOCs) are the same as volatile organic compounds (VOCs), but with methane excluded. Methane is relatively non-toxic and not directly harmful to human health, although it does significantly contribute to climate change and ozone formation. NMVOCs are air pollutants that can harm human health and typically originate from combustion or the evaporation of fuels and other substances.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1751">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1543"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1754"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1753">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q58"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Civil society engagement refers to the involvement of non-governmental organizations, community groups, and other forms of civic participation in public policy-making and advocacy. This form of engagement plays a crucial role in shaping political agendas, representing diverse interests, and ensuring accountability from governmental institutions.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1754">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1426"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1751"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q888"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q889"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q899"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q65"/>
        <cpr:P20 xml:lang="en">6y38j2hv</cpr:P20>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Achieving climate goals will require different jobs; in most cases, it is possible to modify existing jobs, but this will require training in new skills. Aligning skills in this way can be done through a variety of policy interventions. Moreover, many of these green skills overlap with general employable skills, so we include a number of more generic skill improvement initiatives here too.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1757">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1747"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1745"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q684"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1758">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1759"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1760"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1813"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1748"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q684"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1759">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1747"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1748"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1757"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1758"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1744"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q176">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q157"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1883"/>
        <cpr:P4 rdf:resource="https://www.ctc-n.org/technologies/water-recycling-and-reclamation"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1760">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1758"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1803"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1804"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q58"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1763">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1741"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1742"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1827"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1582"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q58"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Transformative governance is a proactive, systemic approach to addressing profound societal and ecological transitions, such as pandemics, climate change, and economic shifts, by fundamentally reshaping existing systems towards a sustainable common good. It crucially involves the inclusive engagement of diverse civil society actors, alongside academia, industry, and government (the &quot;quadruple helix&quot;), in co-creating solutions and driving a just transition that balances social, environmental, and digital dimensions.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1764">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1739"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">The Just Transition Fund (JTF) is a new financial tool under the EU&apos;s cohesion policy, designed to help regions grappling with significant social and economic difficulties as they shift to a climate-neutral economy. Its core purpose is to ensure that the ambitious goals of the European Green Deal (i.e., making the EU climate-neutral by 2050) are met in a fair way, without leaving any communities behind. The JTF offers grants to regions and areas particularly vulnerable to this transition, especially those heavily reliant on fossil fuels like coal, peat, and oil shale, or those with industries that produce a lot of greenhouse gases.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1765">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1562"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q886"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Unemployment benefit schemes provide income support, usually over a limited period, to those who face temporary unemployment. The objective is to provide at least partial income replacement, enabling the beneficiary to maintain a certain standard of living during the transition period until a new employment is available.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1766">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1562"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1809"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Conditional Cash Transfer (CCT) programs are a type of social safety net intervention designed to alleviate poverty and promote human capital development by providing regular financial payments directly to low-income households, contingent upon their fulfillment of specific conditions. These conditions typically relate to investments in human capital, such as ensuring children&apos;s regular school attendance, adhering to preventative health check-ups and vaccinations, and participating in nutrition education or other social development activities. The aim is to provide immediate poverty relief while simultaneously breaking the intergenerational cycle of poverty by fostering long-term improvements in health, education, and overall well-being.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1767">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q53"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1649"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Healthcare in the just transition must also ensure equitable and continuous access to quality healthcare for communities and workers most affected by the economic shifts away from carbon-intensive industries, including robust mental health support, thereby guaranteeing that health outcomes and access to care remain just and do not exacerbate existing or create new inequalities during this profound societal and economic transformation</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1768">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1783"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1784"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1785"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1786"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1787"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1789"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q572"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Solid biofuels include crops and animal products in the form of solids. Solid biofuels also include secondary energy carriers, like charcoal and biomass pellets and briquettes. The IRENA classification of biofuels groups them as primary or secondary products, with primary products also divided by source. Splitting primary solid biofuels by source reflects interest in the environmental impacts of the different ways biological resources enter the bioenergy production chain. In energy statistics, solid biofuels should be reported in tonnes, and the energy content should be reported in joules based on measured net calorific values. Biomass pellets and briquettes, as well as charcoal, can be produced with any primary solid biofuel. The sum of all primary and secondary solid biofuels yields the total solid biofuels. When considering secondary products in the energy balance, transformation processes must be accounted for to avoid double counting or the mixing of higher energy content products with lower-energy unprocessed products. Where detailed statistics about the use of recovered wood products for energy are available, these amounts can be counted in this category if they are not included in renewable waste.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1769">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1782"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q595"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q596"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q572"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Liquid biofuel is mainly used as transport fuel but can also be used for electricity generation (e.g. in diesel generators) or for heating. Liquid biofuels include various alcohols, fatty acid methyl or ethyl esters, synthetic hydrocarbons and vegetable oils used directly as fuel. They are often blended with liquid fossil fuels such as petroleum gasoline and diesel. In these cases, only the biofuel component should be counted as renewable energy. This category comprises biogasoline, biodiesel, bio-jet kerosene and other liquid biofuels. The division into these groups is based on the biofuel’s suitability to blend with similar fossil fuels. Liquid biofuel is primary if the biomass feedstock used to produce it is not part of the energy system and is thus out of the scope of energy statistics (e.g. non-energy crops, waste products or new energy pathways). It is secondary if the biomass feedstock used to produce it is an energy product or biomass that would otherwise be used for energy purposes (e.g. energy crops or wood fuel). When secondary, energy statistics should include the biomass feedstock as a primary energy product, and then record the transformation of the feedstock into the specific liquid or gas biofuel in question.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q177">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q157"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1883"/>
        <cpr:P4 rdf:resource="https://tech-action.unepccc.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2019/05/tna-guidebook-adaptationagriculture.pdf"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1770">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1790"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1791"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1792"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q572"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Gas biofuels are primarily used for heat and power production, but they can also serve as a transport fuel and, in small quantities, as a chemical feedstock for non-energy use. When gas biofuels are mixed with natural gas, only the biological component should be considered as renewable energy. Most gas biofuels are treated as primary energy products, and the energy content of the solid materials used to produce gas biofuels is not recorded in energy balances. Solid biofuels and renewable waste used to manufacture biogas from thermal processes should be shown as a transformation flow, accounting for transformation losses. Division of gas biofuel is based on a mix between the process used to produce them (e.g. anaerobic digestion or thermal processes) and their chemical characteristics (e.g. biomethane, biohydrogen). Gas biolfuels are mostly primary energy products. Gas biofuel is primary if the biomass feedstock used to produce it is not part of the energy system and is thus out of the scope of energy statistics (e.g. non-energy crops, waste products or new energy pathways). It is secondary if the biomass feedstock used to produce it is an energy product or biomass that would otherwise be used for energy purposes (e.g. energy crops or wood fuel). When secondary, energy statistics should include the biomass feedstock as a primary energy product, and then record the transformation of the feedstock into the specific liquid or gas biofuel in question.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1771">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q606"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Water can be pumped into reservoirs used only for the purpose of mixed hydropower (pure pumped hydro), or it can be combined with reservoir hydropower by pumping water into reservoirs that also capture naturally flowing water. Electricity from flowing water that has previously been pumped into reservoirs is not counted as renewable energy but is subtracted from the amount of energy used for pumping, and the net energy consumption is counted as own-use of electricity for the energy industry. During periods of low demand or excess generation from other renewable sources, the plant can use excess electricity to pump water uphill to the reservoir, effectively storing energy for later use. When demand increases or other renewable sources are not available, the stored water can be released to turn turbines and generate electricity. In electricity generation statistics, production from mixed plants is divided into the proportion coming from naturally flowing water and the proportion coming from pumped hydro. The latter is counted as electricity generation from pumped hydro.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1772">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q615"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Most wave energy conversion technologies use floating devices that may or may not be anchored to the seabed. These devices move up and down with the waves, using this movement to push water or compressed air through turbines or convert the fluids’ momentum into rotational motion through rack and pinion mechanisms. Other wave energy converters include devices that capture the water at the tops of waves to create short-term differences in water levels (and use this to drive water turbines) and devices on the seabed that use differences in water pressure to drive turbines as waves pass over them. Specific technologies used to harvest wave energy include (a) point absorbers, which are floating buoys; (b) oscillating wave surge converters, which use an oscillating flap to capture energy while mounted on the seabed; and (c) oscillating water columns, which are partially submerged, hollow columns that compress air pockets through an air turbine.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1773">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q615"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Tidal energy can be subdivided into (a) rise and fall tides and (b) ebb and flood currents. Tides involve ocean water moving vertically due to the gravitational pull of the sun and of the moon, and those pulls’ interactions with Earth’s gravitational field. Tides occur twice a day and depend on the Earth’s location and position. Tidal energy is predictable and is a constant source of energy suitable for producing baseload power. Tidal energy is usually generated close (&lt;20km) to the coastline.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1774">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q615"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Energy is usually harvested from ocean currents in shallow water and driven by wind flows, density changes, salinity and temperature, with the water flowing in one direction either permanently or over long periods of time. Ocean current energy is typically generated more than 20 km offshore at typical depths over 500m. It&apos;s a constant source of energy suitable for baseload electricity generation. In theory, ebb and flood currents are within the scope of ocean currents, but IRENA categorises them as tidal energy due to their proximity to land.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1775">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q615"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Energy produced this way depends on a phenomenon that occurs when two different solutions mix. In this case, the two solutions are fresh water and salt water, for which the main difference is their salinity concentration. The two main mechanisms for generating salinity gradient energy are pressure-retarded osmosis and reversed electrodialysis. In pressure-retarded osmosis, water with a low salt content moves through membranes through osmotic permeation to reduce the salinity of salt water on the other side of the membranes and then expands inside a turbine to generate electricity. In reversed electrodialysis, the ions in salt water are used to generate an electric charge, which can be used as electricity. Other mechanisms for capturing and using these forces, such as capacity mixing, are also being researched. Salinity gradient is especially useful where rivers connect to the open ocean. Salinity gradient electricity generation has transformation losses, so total energy supply and electricity production will differ. However, the efficiency of such systems is usually high, at around 90% for pressure-retarded osmosis.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1776">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q615"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">An organic Rankine thermodynamic cycle can generate electricity using heat transfer between these two water flows. Thermal gradient power is more suitable in places where surface water gets warmer than colder deeper water, such as in tropical regions. As ocean thermal energy conversion systems use thermal energy to generate electricity, there are transformation losses in the system, and the amount of primary energy supplied will be more than the amount of electricity generated. In 2024 generally accepted estimate of what the efficiency of such systems might be existed, but it was expected to be relatively low (under 5%).</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1777">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1780"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1781"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q618"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Solar cells can be arranged together as solar panels and panels can be arranged as arrays. Solar photovoltaic is either connected to an electricity grid or not. Solar photovoltaic devices may also integrate mechanisms, known as concentrator photovoltaic devices, to focus or concentrate solar irradiation onto solar cells, thus increasing their electricity output. High-concentration photovoltaic devices may also need cooling mechanisms for the solar cells. While electricity production from high-concentration photovoltaic devices may resemble concentrated solar power generation, it is classified as solar photovoltaic energy. But any useful heat recovered from cooling water in such devices should be counted in the appropriate subcategory under solar thermal energy. Due to energy access and grid operation planning, the IRENA categorisation subdivides solar photovoltaic into on-grid solar photovoltaic and off-grid solar photovoltaic.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1778">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q620"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Solar water heaters (SWHs) have collectors that absorb the energy from the sun and transfer it to a heat transfer fluid. The heated fluid transfers heat to the domestic water. They are most effective in areas with a high level of solar irradiance and a significant demand for hot water, and they are widely used in residential, commercial and industrial settings.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1779">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q620"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Solar air heating systems typically consist of a collector panel, which absorbs sunlight and heats the air, and potentially a mechanism for circulating the heated air into a building or process. There are three main types of solar air heating process: natural convection, forced convection and radiative heating. The heated air can be used for domestic space heating, ventilation heating, industrial process heating or agricultural drying.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1780">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2498"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2499"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2500"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2652"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1777"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Photovoltaic systems and technologies (e.g. panels, arrays) connected to large-scale electricity networks, allowing excess generation to be fed into the grid. Such systems enable net‐metering or feed‑in tariffs, which may enhance economic viability and encourage distributed energy resources.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1781">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2494"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2495"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2496"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2497"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1777"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1782">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1769"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2766"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2722"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Bio-jet kerosene can be manufactured from any type of biomass, including plant cellulose and algae. It is produced by a range of thermal processes (including gasification followed by Fischer-Tropsch synthesis or pyrolysis followed by hydrogenation) or by the conversion of sugar to hydrocarbons using micro-organisms such as yeast. Jet fuel is a high-specification fuel that must meet the standards defined in ASTM D1655 for Jet A1 or in the Ministry of Defence Standard 91-91 in the United Kingdom. Bio-jet kerosene must meet these same standards and, in addition, have an ASTM D7566 certification. The ASTM certification procedure is rigorous and can take years and millions of US dollars to complete. Bio-jet kerosene may be blended with other fuels or used directly. Where it is blended, energy statisticians should register a transfer or a transformation, from bio-jet kerosene to blended kerosene.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1783">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1768"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Harvested biomass can be used directly or as a feedstock for various bioenergy applications, including combustion for heat and power generation, compression to make biomass pellets and briquettes, and gasification for liquid and gas biofuel production.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1784">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1768"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2550"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1865"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2538"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q572"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Harvest residues can be left on site for nutrient cycling purposes or used to produce liquid biofuels, such as cellulosic ethanol, which can be blended with gasoline to fuel vehicles. Harvest residues can also be burned directly to produce heat and electricity in biomass-fired power plants.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1785">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1768"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Includes by-products obtained during the processing of different types of biomass, such as roundwood or agricultural crops, that have significant energy potential. Since these residues are already collected during the processing of biomass, they can be a convenient and inexpensive source of biomass for energy production. These residues can be used for direct combustion or gasification, or they can be further processed into advanced biofuels, such as bio-oil or biogas. Many of these residues have non-energy uses (e.g. as feed, mulch or animal bedding or for the production of reconstituted wood panels), so only the share of these residues used for energy should be counted in energy statistics.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1786">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1768"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Solid biofuels baed on animal residue consist of by-products from the slaughter and processing of animals. The use of animal processing residues for energy production can provide economic and environmental benefits by reducing waste disposal costs and providing a source of renewable energy. However, their use must comply with regulations regarding waste disposal and public health.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1787">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1768"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Biomass pellets and briquettes are solid, processed biofuels made from dried, cut, crushed and compressed biomass. Wood pellets, created from fuelwood or wood residues, are the most common, but other solid biomass materials such as rice husks, straw, nut shells and renewable municipal waste can also be used. Producing these biofuels involves compressing the biomass or adding a binder, which typically makes up less than 3% of the final weight. These biofuels have low moisture content (below 10%) and high combustion efficiency. Pellets are cylindrical with diameters under 25mm and lengths under 100mm. Briquettes are also cylindrical but can vary in size. Biomass pellets and briquettes are primarily used for heat production (either sold or for direct use) and are increasingly used for electricity generation in countries that rely on imported biofuels to meet bioenergy targets. Torrefied biomass pellets and briquettes, known as black pellets or bio-coal, are not included in this category and should be classified as charcoal. When reporting biomass pellet and briquette use, data on the primary energy sources (solid biofuels) used for production should be included in their respective categories, with the amounts used for manufacturing indicated as other transformation in the energy balance. If available, the renewable content of pellets and briquettes made from refuse-derived fuel and solid recovered fuel can also be included, with the primary input amounts appearing in the statistics for renewable municipal waste.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1788">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1768"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1789">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1768"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Charcoal mainly consists of carbon, with small amounts of ash and other impurities. Charcoal is typically made from wood (fuelwood and wood residues) but can be made from almost any type of solid biomass. Carbonisation usually involves slow pyrolysis, where biomass is heated in a controlled environment without air for several days (for artisanal charcoal). Alternatively, charcoal can be produced more quickly in an industrial kiln or retort that operates at high temperatures. Heat for the charcoal-making process can come from partial combustion of the original biomass or other energy sources. Charcoal is primarily used for direct heat, with cooking and iron and steel production being the most common uses, where it substitutes for coke. It also has non-energy uses, such as in medicine and filters as activated carbon. Charcoal comes in various forms, depending on the type of primary solid biofuel used, including lump charcoal from fuelwood, charcoal briquettes, bamboo charcoal and charcoal made from compressed wood pellets and briquettes. Additionally, torrefied biomass pellets and briquettes are classified as charcoal and can be used as a substitute for coal and for co-firing in thermal power plants. Torrefied biomass is different from charcoal produced through the traditional slow pyrolysis process, since it involves heating the biomass at lower temperatures and for shorter periods of time.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q179">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q178"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1834"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">The ability of a power system to resist, respond, and recover from a catastrophic event - such as environmental disasters - are key factors commonly used to define the resilience of the power system. Resilience measures reduce damage from outages and hasten restoration and recovery to shorten outage durations.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1790">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1770"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2606"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q366"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Anaerobic digestion is a biochemical process that occurs in low-oxygen environments, where organic matter (made up of the elements C-H-O-N-S) decomposes in contact with water to produce methane and carbon dioxide (biogas). The full process consists of four complex biochemical reactions: hydrolysis, acidogenesis, acetogenesis, and methanogenesis. On a large scale, biogases from anaerobic digestion are primarily used for electricity production (including combined heat and power production) or for mixing with natural gas in gas distribution networks. On a small scale, they are more often used to cook (within the heat rising energy sector). When these biogases are blended with other gases, energy statisticians should register this as a transfer or as a transformation from the biogas to the blended gas. If any of the gases are used in the production process, this should be shown as own-use of the gases and not as biogas consumption.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1791">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1770"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Pyrolysis is a thermochemical process where biomass is heated in the absence or near-absence of oxygen to prevent oxidation. This process produces charcoal, pyrolysis gases, and oils. These outputs can be further processed into bioliquids or biogases or used as a heat source for the pyrolysis itself. The yield of solids, liquids, and gases depends on the pyrolysis temperature and residence time—faster pyrolysis favors gas production. Combustion, by contrast, involves burning biomass at high temperatures with excess air (λ &gt; 1), mainly to generate heat. Complete combustion yields CO₂ and water, though incomplete combustion can produce other by-products. Dust is also formed, consisting of coarse fly ash (containing metals and unburned organics) and aerosols—tiny solid and liquid particles. Gasification lies between pyrolysis and combustion. It heats biomass with limited oxygen (λ &lt; 1), producing carbon-rich dust and biogas. Gasification reactors often include combined zones for drying, pyrolysis, combustion, and carbon reduction. The resulting synthesis gas (&quot;syngas&quot;) typically contains CO, CO₂, and H₂, with possible traces of CH₄ and N₂. Its composition depends on the biomass type and process used. Syngas is versatile: it can be used to generate electricity (including combined heat and power), or to produce biomethane, advanced biofuels, and other non-energy chemicals. However, for statistical energy reporting, only the portion of biogas from thermal processes used to generate electricity or biomethane is counted. When biogas is upgraded to biomethane, only the thermal biogas share should be reflected in energy balances, possibly via a transformation flow.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1792">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1770"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Gas biofuel products can be categorise according to chemical composition. Biological feedstock undergoes a chemical or physical transformation process to produce biomethane, biohydrogen, biodimethyl ether and other biogas products.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1793">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q69"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">A job requiring a substantial amount of green skills, largely based on an analysis by Nesta.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1794">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q69"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">a job where a substantial amount of time is spent on green activities, based on an analysis by Nesta</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1795">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q69"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Green versions of jobs in high-emitting industries, created by taking job names from a Nesta analysis and adding green prefixes.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1797">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q858"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q53"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Supported housing is any housing scheme where housing is provided alongside care, support or supervision to help people live as independently as possible in the community. This can include financial aid, practical help with finding accommodation, and support in maintaining their tenancy.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1799">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1800"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1801"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q71"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q53"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q65"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q18">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1851"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q16"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q17"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q19"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q4"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q180">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q178"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1968"/>
        <cpr:P4 rdf:resource="https://www.unescap.org/sites/default/files/14.%20FS-Decentralized-energy-system.pdf"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1800">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1799"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1801">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1799"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1803">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1760"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1804"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q684"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Indigenous Peoples Plan (IPPs) is a measure to approach Indigenous People and guarantee provisions to mitigate or avoid adverse impacts on indigenous groups. The IPP is a management plan that specifies responsibilities, timeframes, budgetary considerations, monitoring and evaluation agreements as well as engagement and mitigation strategies.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1804">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1760"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q684"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Indigenous Peoples Planning Frameworks provide guidance on safeguard screening, assessment, institutional arrangements, and processes to be followed for subprojects or subsequent tranches that are prepared after Board approval.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1805">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1806"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q47"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1806">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1805"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">The objective of the Ezcazú Agreement is to guarantee the full and effective implementation in Latin America and the Caribbean of the rights of access to environmental information, public participation in the environmental decision-making process and access to justice in environmental matters, and the creation and strengthening of capacities and cooperation, contributing to the protection of the right of every person of present and future generations to live in a healthy environment and to sustainable development</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1807">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1744"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1809">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1766"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q886"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Payments of money to people so that they can buy what they need. These programmes are often used in a crisis, but cash assistance programs are also increasingly seen as a simple to implement tool for social protection.</cpr:P7>
        <cpr:P9 xml:lang="en">non-cash benefits</cpr:P9>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1810">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1823"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1815"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1816"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">GRMs are defined as organizational systems and resources established by national government agencies (or, as appropriate, by regional or municipal agencies) to receive and address concerns about the impact of their policies, programs and operations on external stakeholders. GRMs are intended to be accessible, collaborative, expeditious, and effective in resolving concerns through dialogue, joint fact-finding, negotiation, and problem solving</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1811">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q53"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Energy Assistance, in its most basic sense, is about providing crucial support to individuals and households to afford essential energy services, ensuring their basic needs are met.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1812">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q650"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Any form or mixture of gases produced in refineries by distillation, cracking, reforming, and other processes. The principal constituents are methane and ethane. May contain hydrogen and small/trace amounts of other gases. Still gas is typically consumed as refinery fuel or used as petrochemical feedstock. Still gas burned for refinery fuel may differ in composition from marketed still gas sold to other users.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1813">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1758"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1824"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1825"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1733"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1814">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1823"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1815"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1816"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Environmental and Social Management System (ESMS) is a framework that helps organizations integrate environmental and social objectives into their core business operations through a set of clearly defined and repeatable processes. Its main purpose is to identify and manage a project&apos;s potential environmental and social risks and impacts, ensuring compliance with relevant laws, regulations, and international standards.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1815">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1823"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1810"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1814"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1816"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Environmental and Social Impact Assessment (ESIA) predicts the environmental and social consequences that a future project/intervention might entail. It is carried out before project implementation and proposes measures to mitigate potential negative impacts. An ESIA has two aims: 1) to minimise or avoid adverse environmental and social effects before they occur; 2) to integrate environmental and social concerns into decision-making.  ESIA ought to evaluate alternatives, design appropriate mitigation, management and monitoring measures.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1816">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1823"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1810"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1814"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1815"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">The Environmental and Social Management Plan (ESMP) consists of the set of mitigation,monitoring and institutional measures to be taken during the design, construction and operation stages of the project to eliminate adverse environmental and social impacts, to offset them, or to reduce them to acceptable levels. The plan also includes the actions needed for the implementation of these measures. The ESMP will establish measures to prevent and mitigate the risks identified in the Environmental and Social Impact Assessment (ESIA)</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1817">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1306"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1307"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1311"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1489"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q641"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q642"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q643"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q644"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q647"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q733"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q738"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q639"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Petroleum is a broader term encompassing both crude oil (the unrefined, raw form) and refined petroleum products like gasoline and diesel.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1818">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q639"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Oil shale is a sedimentary rock containing kerogen, a solid organic matter. Different from shale oil which is liquid petroleum produced by heating shale oil.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
