        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q146"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1847"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1848"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1849"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1850"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1851"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1853"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q19"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1974"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2015"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q21"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1831"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2801"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Terrestrial ecosystem-based adaptation are land-based strategies and activities for adapting to climate change that harness nature-based solutions and ecosystem services. For instance, reforestation can hold back desertification and recharge groundwater supplies in times of drought.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1847">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1846"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2821"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">A thin strip of vegetation used by wildlife and potentially allowing movement of biotic factors between two areas.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1848">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1635"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1846"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2543"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2794"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1406"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1921"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Inland wetland restoration is the manipulation of a former or degraded inland wetland&apos;s physical, chemical, or biological characteristics to return its natural functions. Restoration practices include re-establishment, the rebuilding a former wetland; and rehabilitation, repairing the functions of a degraded wetland and making it more resilient to climate change.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1849">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1846"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2417"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2543"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q786"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Grassland restoration and rehabilitation include activities to preserve or restore grasslands for agricultural, conservation and carbon storage reasons. Restoration of these grasslands may benefit some mammal species that are associated with them.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q185">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q181"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q182"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q372"/>
        <cpr:P4 rdf:resource="https://resilientmaritimelogistics.unctad.org/guidebook/14-climate-change"/>
        <cpr:P4 rdf:resource="https://resilientmaritimelogistics.unctad.org/guidebook/21-defining-port-resilience"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1850">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1846"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2539"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2790"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q786"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Avoided grassland and shrubland conversion refers to activities and measures to prevent grassland and shrubland being converted to other land uses, such as agriculture and crop production, or infrastructure, maintaining ecosystem resilience to climate change. Grassland and shrubland soils are significant reservoirs of organic carbon that will, if left uncultivated, continue to store this carbon below ground.  Grassland and shrubland ecosystems may also support greater plant biomass than annual cropland, especially below ground.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1851">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q17"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q18"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2541"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1846"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2539"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2659"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1404"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q4"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q786"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Avoided forest conversion refers to activities that avoid deforestation and maintain the integrity and resilience of forested ecosystems. This maintains carbon stocks and forest resilience to climate change. Activities include designation of conservation easements on forested land, the use of protective guidelines, such as best management practices, that avoid unintentional loss of forest cover or soil carbon and reducing the amount of forest that is displaced for forest management activities (e.g. designing road systems or landings to minimize their footprint).</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1853">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q132"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2001"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2660"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2800"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1586"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1845"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1846"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2659"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2795"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q787"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q134"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1586"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Forest stewardship is a general approach to forest management that meets the needs of current owners but doesn&apos;t detract from or degrade the use by future generations. Forest stewardship is based on conservation principles that ensure protection of all forest resources including wildlife, timber, soil, water recreational opportunities and makes them more resilient to climate change. Forest stewards actively manage their land on a long-term basis by following management objectives that are based on multiple resources, are economically viable and conserve natural resources.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1854">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1845"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q979"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Rainguards protect rubber tree tapping, helping to maintain latex rubber tapping yields and prevent the growth of fungi during periods of rainfall.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1855">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2667"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q352"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q131"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1845"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2417"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2659"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1864"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Agroforestry refers to land use systems in which trees are grown in combination with agriculture on the same land. Agroforestry exploits the complementarity between perennial species (trees or shrubs) and crops, so that the available resources can be more effectively exploited. Efficient and modern versions of agroforestry allow the diversification of farm activity and make better use of environmental resources. Agroforestry can be implemented in different regions, producing food and fibre for better food and nutritional security, sustaining livelihoods, alleviating poverty and promoting productive, resilient agricultural environments. Moreover, agroforestry can contribute to climate change adaptation by preventing deforestation, increasing biodiversity conservation, producing cleaner water and controlling soil erosion, thus enabling agricultural lands to better cope with floods and drought events.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1856">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q131"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Climate-resilient crop storage refers to methods to store crops post harvest and ensure they remain in good condition that can withstand future climate impacts.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1857">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q131"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Ex situ or ‘off-site’ conservation means the conservation of components of biological diversity outside their natural habitats. The main objective of ex situ techniques is to support conservation by ensuring the survival of threatened species and the maintenance of associated genetic diversity. Ex situ techniques should always be seen as complimentary to in situ conservation, aiding species recovery and reintroduction. The main techniques are: gene banks, e.g. seed banks, sperm and ova banks; captive breeding of animals and artificial propagation of plants; and collections of living organisms for zoos, aquaria, and botanic gardens for research and public awareness.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1858">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1873"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2579"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Food surplus distribution refers to increasing the availability of food by enabling products to flow from surplus to deficit areas, which can emerge as a consequence of extreme weather events or other disruptions. It is a practice used to reduce emissions related to food waste management and adapt to climate-related food insecurity.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1860">
