        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1832"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2012">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1635"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1882"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1832"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2013">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1838"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Extrapolating the effects of large-scale climate processes to regional and local scales of interest. Statistical downscaling encompasses the use of various statistics-based techniques to determine relationships between large-scale climate patterns resolved by global climate models and observed local climate responses. These relationships are applied to GCM results to transform climate model outputs into statistically refined products, often considered to be more appropriate for use as input to regional or local climate impacts studies.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2015">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1586"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1846"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2016">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1886"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2017">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1927"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2018">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1883"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Boreholes and tubewells are important adaptation technology measures for providing a domestic water supply during times of water shortages and drought. They extract freshwater from subsurface or deeper groundwater aquifers.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2019">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q115"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q82"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1835"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2020">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1836"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2021">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1830"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2022">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1830"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Technologies play a key role in adapting to climate change, helping countries and societies respond to a changing environment and managing both current and future climate risks. However, developing countries face barriers to tap into these new technologies. Adaptation technology transfer refers to the action of transferring technologies that aid climate adaptation to developing countries, accelerating their capacity to adapt to climate change.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2023">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1830"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2024">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1644"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1888"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1833"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2026">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1836"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Measures and actions to ensure that human development is sustainable and resilient to the impacts of climate change and related disaster risks.</cpr:P7>
        <cpr:P9 xml:lang="en">carbon neutral &amp; climate resilient development</cpr:P9>
        <cpr:P9 xml:lang="en">low GHG emissions and climate-resilient development</cpr:P9>
        <cpr:P9 xml:lang="en">low carbon climate resilient development</cpr:P9>
        <cpr:P9 xml:lang="en">low emissions, resilient development</cpr:P9>
        <cpr:P9 xml:lang="en">low greenhouse gas and climate resilient development</cpr:P9>
        <cpr:P9 xml:lang="en">low greenhouse gas emissions and climate resilient development</cpr:P9>
        <cpr:P9 xml:lang="en">low greenhouse gas emissions and climate-resilient development</cpr:P9>
        <cpr:P9 xml:lang="en">low- emission and climate-resilient development</cpr:P9>
        <cpr:P9 xml:lang="en">low-carbon and climate resilient development</cpr:P9>
        <cpr:P9 xml:lang="en">low-carbon and climate- resilient development</cpr:P9>
        <cpr:P9 xml:lang="en">low-carbon and/or climate-resilient development</cpr:P9>
        <cpr:P9 xml:lang="en">low-carbon, adapted and resilient development</cpr:P9>
        <cpr:P9 xml:lang="en">low-emission and climate resilient development</cpr:P9>
        <cpr:P9 xml:lang="en">low-emission and climate-resilient development</cpr:P9>
        <cpr:P9 xml:lang="en">low-emission resilient development</cpr:P9>
        <cpr:P9 xml:lang="en">sustainable and climate-resilient development</cpr:P9>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2029">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q393"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q980"/>
        <cpr:P20 xml:lang="en">ghax83yn</cpr:P20>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Hot extremes are defined as the daily maximum temperatures that were exceeded on average once in a decade (10-year event) or once in 50 years (50-year event) during the 1850–1900 reference period. These extremes refer to unusually high temperatures, including heatwaves, that are increasing in intensity and frequency due to human-induced climate change. This includes both heatwaves (over land) and marine heatwaves (over sea).</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2030">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q695"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q704"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2031">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q695"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q21">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1846"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q787"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q19"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q23"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q4"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q560"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Afforestation is the establishment of forest through planting, deliberate seeding or natural regeneration on land that, until then, was under a different land use or not used. Afforestation implies a transformation of land use from non-forest to forest. Afforestation can help forests to adapt to climate change by decreasing human pressures (for example by reducing the destruction or degradation of habitats) and enhancing landscape connectivity and reducing fragmentation (thus facilitating species migration under climate change conditions). Afforestation may also contribute to preserve biodiversity hotspots, avoiding soil degradation and protecting other natural resources (e.g. water).</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q221">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q218"/>
        <cpr:P20 xml:lang="en">zahr3y3c</cpr:P20>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1652"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q226"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q4"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q638"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q639"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q661"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q716"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q754"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q770"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Greenhouse gas emissions are the release of heat-trapping gases—such as carbon dioxide (CO₂), methane (CH₄), nitrous oxide (N₂O), and fluorinated gases—into the atmosphere, primarily through human activities like fossil fuel combustion, industrial processes, deforestation, and agriculture, which enhance the natural greenhouse effect and drive climate change.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q223">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q218"/>
        <cpr:P20 xml:lang="en">dntrzvg4</cpr:P20>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q257"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q370"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q5"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q560"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q638"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q641"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q650"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q661"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q771"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Carbon dioxide (CO₂) is a naturally-occurring, colourless and odourless gas that is a critical component of Earth&apos;s carbon cycle that is vital for processes like photosynthesis. Its excessive accumulation from human activities such as burning fossil fuels, deforestation, and industrial processes intensifies the greenhouse effect and drives climate change.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q226">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1009"/>
        <cpr:P20 xml:lang="en">d2nyvpp8</cpr:P20>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q221"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q638"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q645"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q650"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q670"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Methane (CH₄) is a potent greenhouse gas that, while present in smaller concentrations than carbon dioxide, has a much higher short-term warming potential - emitted from both natural sources like wetlands and human activities such as fossil fuel extraction, livestock production, and waste management - making it a critical driver of climate change. It is also a precursor to the formation of tropospheric ozone.</cpr:P7>
        <cpr:P9 xml:lang="en">non-methane</cpr:P9>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q229">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q218"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q638"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q786"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Nitrous oxide is a greenhouse gas 300 times more potent than carbon dioxide (CO2) that remains in the atmosphere for more than 100 years.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q237">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q218"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q235"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">CFCs have been widely used in refrigeration, air conditioning, packaging, insulation, and aerosol sprays. Because they do not break down in the lower atmosphere, CFCs rise into the upper atmosphere, where they damage the ozone layer. They are also a greenhouse gases that contribute to climate change.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
