    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2667">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1855"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q362"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Silvopasture is a category of agroforestry in which trees are integrated with grazing animals to create a managed woodland pasture. There are two ways to achieve a silvopasture, either by planting trees in a pasture or by thinning a forest enough to establish pasture plants.  The aim is to secure the benefits that trees bring but to manage them so they do not over-shade the pasture. In typical silvopastural systems, the trees are productive; either producing and timber crop or a fruit or nut harvest. Benefits include improvements in soil health and animal welfare.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
