        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q818"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q856"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q978"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q979"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q983"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Earth&apos;s temperature has risen by an average of 0.11° Fahrenheit (0.06° Celsius) per decade since 1850 due to human induced climate change. Increasing temperatures intensify the hydrologic cycle, causing dry regions to become drier and wet regions to become wetter.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q977">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q218"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q979">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q374"/>
        <cpr:P20 xml:lang="en">n3a3av8a</cpr:P20>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1004"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1854"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q974"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q982"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Precipitation refers to any form of water falling from the sky, such as rain, snow, sleet, or hail. Extreme precipitation, influenced by both thermodynamic and dynamic processes, has increased due to warming and other factors. Since the mid-20th century, maximum daily precipitation over land has risen, with greenhouse gases attributed as dominant contributor.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q98">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q100"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q96"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q99"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1838"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q83"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q104"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q84"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q86"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q87"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q905"/>
        <cpr:P4 rdf:resource="https://maps.scag.ca.gov/climate/?page=page_3"/>
        <cpr:P4 rdf:resource="https://www.ctc-n.org/sites/www.ctc-n.org/files/resources/climate_change_vulnerability_assessments_1.pdf"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Climate change vulnerability assessments evaluate how climate change will impact a specific system (e.g., a river basin, water sector, country, or city). These assessments go beyond the exposure (e.g. rainfall) by also considering the system&apos;s characteristics (sensitivity) and its ability to adapt (adaptive capacity). The results help to identify near-term actions to prioritise, as well as identify those the longer term issues.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q980">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2029"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q389"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q374"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Temperature extremes refer to significant deviations from average temperatures, including both unusually high (warm) and low (cold) extremes. Influenced predominantly by greenhouse gases, these extremes are assessed globally, continentally, and regionally, focusing on changes in intensity, frequency, and duration from daily to seasonal scales.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q981">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q382"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1515"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Coastal flooding is the inundation of normally dry land in coastal areas caused by storm surges, high winds, and high tides raising sea levels due to low atmospheric pressure. This flooding can obstruct river flow and is exacerbated by seabed shallowing and reduced return flow. Estuarine flooding involves similar processes and can also be triggered by tsunamis.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q982">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q382"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q106"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q979"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Riverine flooding occurs when rivers and streams overflow their banks, flooding nearby land. This happens due to the complex interplay of hydrology, climate, and human management. Factors like heavy rain, snowmelt, and river management practices contribute to this type of flood.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q983">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1006"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1013"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1014"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q406"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q439"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q441"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q949"/>
        <cpr:P20 xml:lang="en">dsxc3m8e</cpr:P20>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1518"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q372"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q974"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">The cryosphere is the frozen part of the Earth—it includes sea ice, glaciers, snow, and ice sheets. It helps regulate the climate and weather by reflecting sunlight. Rising temperatures are causing melting, leading to sea ice loss, glacier collapse, and glacier landslides and avalanches. This threatens the livelihoods of around 700 million people who rely on the cryosphere for food, water security, and cultural practices.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q984">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q443"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Malnutrition arises from insufficient food intake, poor health, and inadequate care, leading to being underweight, stunted, wasted, or deficient in essential vitamins and minerals. Extreme weather events like floods and droughts can worsen food insecurity and malnutrition by disrupting food production, increasing food prices, and reducing household incomes.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q985">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1215"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1216"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1217"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1218"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1219"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1220"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1221"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1222"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1223"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q954"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q838"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Data security involves protecting data to ensure its availability, confidentiality, and integrity. Impacts to data security include risks from increased system complexity, which can enable cyberattacks, as well as potential data loss from severe incidents like natural disasters</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q986">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1118"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1152"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1161"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1304"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q408"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q987"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q988"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q975"/>
        <cpr:P20 xml:lang="en">mjaupkch</cpr:P20>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Geohazards are adverse geological phenomenon&apos;s that are capable of causing widespread damage or loss to property, life and the environment, such as earthquakes, volcano eruptions and tsunamis. Climate change can increase the frequency and severity of some geohazards, such as landslides, and soil erosion.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q987">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1079"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1080"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1122"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1123"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1125"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1126"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1134"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1136"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1137"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1150"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1174"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q645"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q986"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1143"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q988">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1082"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1085"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1091"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1094"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1119"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1120"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1121"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1151"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1173"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q986"/>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q989">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1187"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1188"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1190"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1191"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1192"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1193"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1194"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1195"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q901"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q954"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Industrial accidents, such as oil spills from leaks, blowouts, or shipping accidents, pose severe climate risks to ecosystems. These incidents, often exacerbated by extreme weather, threaten biodiversity and disrupt ecosystem health. As climate-related extreme events rise, so does the risk of industrial accidents, including oil spills and explosions.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q99">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q83"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q98"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">A qualitative or quantitative approach to determine the nature and extent of disaster risk by analysing potential hazards and evaluating existing conditions of exposure and vulnerability that together could harm people, property, services, livelihoods and the environment on which they depend.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q990">
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1210"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1211"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1212"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1213"/>
        <cpr:P1 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1214"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q954"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Extreme weather events can increase the risks of accidents, disruptions in travel, and lead to road closures or detours, causing delays and logistical challenges.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q991">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q954"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">The acronym CBRNE stands for Chemical, Biological, Radiological, Nuclear, and Explosive. It encompasses a range of hazardous materials and situations, often used in the context of emergency response and public safety to describe threats or incidents involving these dangerous agents.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q992">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q956"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Conflict risk refers to the increased likelihood of social or political unrest arising from various stressors, including social, economic, and environmental factors. While climate change doesn&apos;t directly cause conflict, it can worsen conditions like resource scarcity, food insecurity, and displacement, increasing tensions and instability, especially in regions with poor governance.</cpr:P7>
        <cpr:P9 xml:lang="en">conflict of interest</cpr:P9>
        <cpr:P9 xml:lang="en">conflict of interests</cpr:P9>
        <cpr:P9 xml:lang="en">conflicts of interest</cpr:P9>
        <cpr:P9 xml:lang="en">conflicts of interests</cpr:P9>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q994">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1017"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1167"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1599"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1826"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Food insecurity refers to the lack of reliable access to sufficient, safe, and nutritious food. Climate change exacerbates this issue by directly impacting food production, increasing food prices, and reducing household incomes. In addition, climate change frequently acts to compound drivers of food insecurity, such as poverty and inequity.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q995">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q975"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Extraterrestrial  hazards are caused by asteroids, meteoroids, and comets as they pass near to the Earth, enter the Earth’s atmosphere, and/or strike the Earth, and by changes in interplanetary conditions that effect the Earth’s magnetosphere, ionosphere, and thermosphere.</cpr:P7>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">We have not applied extra-terrestrial hazard to our dataset, as it is not relevant to our climate-centred dataset (e.g. meteor impacts).</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q996">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q368"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Ocean deoxygenation is the loss of oxygen in ocean waters due to warming, reducing oxygen saturation, and increasing consumption, stratification, and ventilation changes. Human activities, such as nutrient runoff and pollution, have worsened coastal and open-ocean hypoxia, harming marine organisms and shrinking marine habitats.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q997">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1017"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1167"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q441"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q818"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q905"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Water scarcity can be defined as a lack of sufficient water, or not having access to safe water supplies, which is worsened by climate change by disrupting precipitation patterns and the water cycle. Currently, two billion people lack safe drinking water, and about half the world’s population faces severe water scarcity for at least part of the year, as climate impacts intensify.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q999">
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q368"/>
        <cpr:P2 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q974"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q435"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">The ocean absorbs excess heat from greenhouse gas emissions, causing rising temperatures that lead to coral bleaching and the loss of breeding grounds for marine species.</cpr:P7>
    </rdf:Description>
</rdf:RDF>



